Calibration of all tested model formulations was achieved using three MCMC chains. Convergence diagnostics indicated satisfactory mixing and convergence overall, with chains converging toward similar posterior distributions and Gelman–Rubin statistics close to 1 ($\hat{R}$). However, for some model formulations, posterior estimates of specific parameters approached the upper or lower bounds of their biologically constrained priors, suggesting limited parameter identifiability or weak support for these formulations. Model comparison based on BIC revealed substantial differences among formulations (@tbl-ResModels).

Preliminary model comparisons supported the stepwise selection of candidate formulations. Models assuming a fixed allocation rule throughout the life cycle (F) systematically produced poorer fits and overestimated post-maturation growth in grouped adults, whereas formulations including a progressive transition in allocation after maturation (@eq-chap3kappaC, C) better reproduced the adult growth plateau. Models using the classical hyperbolic functional response (@eq-chap3funres, Y) also yielded substantially higher BIC values than those using the normalized formulation (@eq-chap3funres, K), as predicted assimilation remained insufficient to reproduce observed growth trajectories across experiments. The formulation in which reserve contribution to fresh body mass scaled with structure (L.Y.F.w) was evaluated only once because this assumption was weakly supported by Bart2019_XP3. In this experiment, alternating between soil supplemented with horse dung and soil alone produced only limited changes in fresh body mass, whereas the structure-dependent formulation implied a stronger influence of reserve dynamics on body mass (@eq-chap3RLW, w). Consistently, this formulation performed less well than those assuming that reserve contribution was independent of structural size (@eq-chap3RLW, e). Subsequent comparisons therefore focused mainly on formulations combining a progressive allocation transition (@eq-chap3kappaC, C) with reserve independence (@eq-chap3RLW, e), while retaining selected fixed energy allocation (F) formulations as reference models.

The two best-supported models shared three common characteristics: (i) a progressive change in energy allocation after maturation (@eq-chap3kappaC, C), (ii) a normalized hyperbolic functional response (@eq-chap3funres, K), and (iii) a formulation in which reserve contribution to fresh body mass was independent of structural size (@eq-chap3RLW, e). The only difference between these two formulations concerned the scaling of the maximum searching rate, which was either structural-mass-specific (@eq-chap3Assim, W) or surface-area-specific (@eq-chap3Assim, L) : model W.K.C.e (BIC = 92.2) and model L.K.C.e (BIC = 46.0). whereas all other formulations had considerably higher BIC values (99-551).

Models assuming a fixed allocation rule throughout the life cycle (F) systematically resulted in poorer fits. Similarly, models using the classical hyperbolic formulation for the functional response (Y) showed much higher BIC values than those using the normalized formulation (K). In practice, these formulations systematically failed to reproduce observed growth trajectories, with predicted assimilation remaining insufficient to sustain realistic growth across experiments. Models assuming reserve contribution proportional to structure (w) also performed less well than formulations assuming reserve independence (e) (see Supplementary information Section S2).

Although the two best models had substantially different BIC values ($\Delta$BIC = 46.2), both provided qualitatively similar predictions for most datasets. However, they greatly differed for the key experiment Bart2019_XP3, where earthworms were alternately fed with soil only and soil supplemented with horse dung. The model with a structural-mass-specific maximum searching rate (W.K.C.e) reproduced the observed growth trajectory more accurately during the final phase of the experiment.This difference was reflected by a lower weight RMSE for the W.K.C.e compared with L.K.C.e (0.035 vs. 0.071, respectively). Consequently, despite a higher overall BIC, W.K.C.e was retained for subsequent analyses because it provided a substantially better fit for Bart2019_XP3 while producing similar fits for the other experiments (@fig-pred_D_CWKe & fig-pred_F_CLKe).

Parameter estimates for the retained model are presented in @tbl-chap3Param. Parameter values were generally consistent between W.K.C.e and L.K.C.e, with as expected, the main differences concerning the ingestion-related parameters $[\dot{F}_m]$, $\{\dot{F}_m\}$ and $K$. For both formulations, posterior estimates of the maximum searching rate approached the upper bound of the prior distribution.

Although the retained model adequately reproduced the main growth and reproduction trajectories across experiments (W.K.C.e, @fig-pred_D_CWKe), some condition-specific deviations remained. For body mass, grouped individuals under low-food conditions (Bart2019_XP4_2) showed an overestimation of the adult plateau, whereas the adult plateau was well estimated for grouped individuals under high-food conditions (Bart2019_XP1_2). This pattern was also observed with the L.K.C.e model. In the density experiments at densities 2 and 5 (Gollot2026_D2 and Gollot2026_D5), the transition between juvenile growth and the adult plateau remained more abrupt in simulations than in observations, despite the introduction of a progressive allocation transition over the timescale parameter ($\tau$). In contrast, this transition was more accurately reproduced for individuals at density 2 with increased food supply (Gollot2026_D2b).

For reproduction, the main deviations also corresponded to specific experimental conditions rather than to a general model bias. For grouped individuals under low-food conditions (Bart2019_XP4_2), observed reproduction decreased when density increased from three to six individuals per cosm, whereas the model predicted similar reproductive outputs between the two conditions. Reproduction was also slightly underestimated for grouped individuals at high-food conditions (Bart2019_XP1_2). For individuals at density 2 with increased food supply (Gollot2026_D2b), reproductive output was overestimated, with observed reproduction remaining substantially lower than predicted by the model.

